he probe (the program whose `daemon` subcommand manages the local app-server daemon, which hosts interactive sessions by default: `codex --help` says `--no-daemon` runs \"without the shared background server\"): in one terminal `env CODEX_HOME=<home> codex app-server --listen unix:///tmp/qbrain-p02-codex-<utc>/app.sock`; in a second, `env CODEX_HOME=<home> codex --remote unix:///tmp/qbrain-p02-codex-<utc>/app.sock -s read-only -a never -C /home/sharpninja/github/McpServer`, where the operator trusts the observer hooks with `/hooks` (Codex hooks documentation; that this works over `--remote` is untested, and if the hooks then do not run, run 2 cannot be performed) and sends prompt A `Run the shell command printenv CODEX_THREAD_ID, then the shell command sleep 90, then reply with the printenv output only` (run 2 records whether that output equals A's `session_id`); while A's `sleep 90` runs, one prompt B per way a message reaches a running session, each with its own text (`Reply with exactly: done B1`, `done B2`, and so on) so each observer line is attributable: each way the TUI offers for a message during a running turn (its on-screen hint names them), and, from a third terminal, `env CODEX_HOME=<home> codex queue --remote unix:///tmp/qbrain-p02-codex-<utc>/app.sock --thread <A's session_id from the observer, used as the \"Session UUID\" that `--thread` takes; if `codex queue` refuses it, that way fails> --message \"Reply with exactly: done Bq\"` (`codex queue --help`: \"Queue a message for an existing session\"); then exit. For each way, B's prompt event fires at enqueue when its UserPromptSubmit `observedAtUtc` is earlier than the `observedAtUtc` of the PostToolUse line for A's `sleep 90` (the PostToolUse line whose command is `sleep 90`) (B was sent while that command ran, so no model step could have taken B in yet), and only when processed otherwise; a B whose prompt event fired at enqueue but that runs as its own later turn (a Stop line carrying B's `turn_id` after A's Stop) also counts as only when processed (queued-message arrival rule, host binding table); a way passes only when its B produced exactly one UserPromptSubmit line (a second line with another `turn_id` would mint a second turn), whose `turn_id` differs from A's and from every other B's, because codex's nonce is `turn_id` (host binding table) and a shared `turn_id` would merge B into A's turn (when a way fires only when processed, P0.2 also records whether the transcript at the payload's `transcript_path` records the enqueue, with its observed payload, for the operator). The codex row fails or is unavailable when run 1 records no UserPromptSubmit line or a payload without `session_id` or `turn_id`, when a way's B, the `codex queue` one included, produces no UserPromptSubmit line, more than one, or one that shares a `turn_id`, or when run 2 cannot be performed; a failed or unavailable codex row, or a result of only when processed for any way, stops P0 and the operator is shown the receipt, because every HV reviewer must persist its own turn through the Codex plugin (host binding table, codex), so every codex arrival path ends either verified or decided by the operator. Afterwards P0.2 copies `observed.jsonl` and run 1's stdout, redacted by the receipt rule of the hostile validation protocol, to `docs/receipts/p0/codex-hook-observer-<utc>.jsonl` and `docs/receipts/p0/codex-hook-probe-exec-<utc>.jsonl`, stops the private app server, signs the probe home out (`env CODEX_HOME=<home> codex logout`), deletes `/tmp/qbrain-p02-codex-<utc>/` (the directory it created), and confirms that `~/.codex/config.toml` still has the recorded SHA-256 and that no file under `~/.codex` contains the probe directory's path (otherwise it stops and shows the operator what changed), so the probe leaves no Codex setting, credential, or path-bearing record in the operator's Codex home; the UserPromptSubmit lines of `codex-hook-observer-<utc>.jsonl` are the recorded codex event payloads that A3's per-host mint cases, A4's mid-turn case, TEST-MCP-PLUGIN-013-AC001, and A9's codex hook cases replay, and that receipt is codex's feature-matrix receipt (P0.8, A9); these runs spawn no subagent, so codex's subagent marker is not observed, and P0.8 records it as unverified in codex's feature-matrix row, the limitation FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-002-AC001 provides for; (d) grok: whether `GROK_SESSION_ID` is set in shell commands, and the same enqueue-versus-processing check for UserPromptSubmit; (e) grok-bot: the Grok Bot cloud runtime's hook payload fields, session environment for shell commands, and queued-message behavior; (f) copilot: two real deliveries of one prompt from two registered hooks and whether their `timestamp` values match (evidence only, D11), two identical prompts sent back to back and one sent while a turn runs with whether their `timestamp` values differ, and whether one message ever reaches the hook twice (a host that gives separate messages equal fields or redelivers in normal use is recorded as a D7 limitation, since its sessions fail closed at the first collision), and the same enqueue-versus-processing check for UserPromptSubmit; (g) cline (classic): whether the installed Cline build runs native hooks, where hook scripts are installed, the UserPromptSubmit payload fields, whether a queued message fires the hook at arrival, and the same equal-field timestamp and redelivery probe as copilot; (h) cline-v2: whether a queued message starts its own run at arrival, and if not, whether a message-queue event exists; (i) opencode: whether `chat.message` fires when a message is created while the session is busy, or only when it is processed. For (d)-(i), the enqueue-versus-processing check records the event's time against the running turn's progress and whether the message then runs inside the running turn or as its own later turn, read from the order of the host's stop or stop-equivalent events (the `turn_id` criterion of (c) is codex's alone). For eve